        <div class="c-article-body">
            <section aria-labelledby="Abs1" data-title="Abstract" lang="en"><div class="c-article-section" id="Abs1-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item" id="Abs1">Abstract</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Abs1-content"><p>Atmospheric methane (CH<sub>4</sub>) is a potent greenhouse gas, and its mole fraction has more than doubled since the preindustrial era<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 1" title="Meinshausen, M. et al. Historical greenhouse gas concentrations for climate modelling (CMIP6). Geosci. Model Dev. 10, 2057–2116 (2017)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR1" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e714">1</a></sup>. Fossil fuel extraction and use are among the largest anthropogenic sources of CH<sub>4</sub> emissions, but the precise magnitude of these contributions is a subject of debate<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2" title="Saunois, M. et al. The global methane budget 2000–2012. Earth Syst. Sci. Data 8, 697–751 (2016)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR2" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e720">2</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 3" title="Schwietzke, S. et al. Upward revision of global fossil fuel methane emissions based on isotope database. Nature 538, 88–91 (2016); corrigendum 543, 452 (2017)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR3" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e723">3</a></sup>. Carbon-14 in CH<sub>4</sub> (<sup>14</sup>CH<sub>4</sub>) can be used to distinguish between fossil (<sup>14</sup>C-free) CH<sub>4</sub> emissions and contemporaneous biogenic sources; however, poorly constrained direct <sup>14</sup>CH<sub>4</sub> emissions from nuclear reactors have complicated this approach since the middle of the 20th century<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 4" title="Lassey, K. R., Etheridge, D. M., Lowe, D. C., Smith, A. M. &amp; Ferretti, D. F. Centennial evolution of the atmospheric methane budget: what do the carbon isotopes tell us? Atmos. Chem. Phys. 7, 2119–2139 (2007)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR4" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e743">4</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 5" title="Zazzeri, G., Acuña Yeomans, E. &amp; Graven, H. D. Global and regional emissions of radiocarbon from nuclear power plants from 1972 to 2016. Radiocarbon 60, 1067–1081 (2018)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR5" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e746">5</a></sup>. Moreover, the partitioning of total fossil CH<sub>4</sub> emissions (presently 172 to 195 teragrams CH<sub>4</sub> per year)<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2" title="Saunois, M. et al. The global methane budget 2000–2012. Earth Syst. Sci. Data 8, 697–751 (2016)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR2" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e754">2</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 3" title="Schwietzke, S. et al. Upward revision of global fossil fuel methane emissions based on isotope database. Nature 538, 88–91 (2016); corrigendum 543, 452 (2017)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR3" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e757">3</a></sup> between anthropogenic and natural geological sources (such as seeps and mud volcanoes) is under debate; emission inventories suggest that the latter account for about 40 to 60 teragrams CH<sub>4</sub> per year<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="Etiope, G. Natural Gas Seepage: The Earth's Hydrocarbon Degassing Vol. 1 (Springer International Publishing, 2015)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e763">6</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 7" title="Etiope, G., Ciotoli, G., Schwietzke, S. &amp; Schoell, M. Gridded maps of geological methane emissions and their isotopic signature. Earth Syst. Sci. Data 11, 1–22 (2019)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR7" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e766">7</a></sup>. Geological emissions were less than 15.4 teragrams CH<sub>4</sub> per year at the end of the Pleistocene, about 11,600 years ago<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 8" title="Petrenko, V. V. et al. Minimal geological methane emissions during the Younger Dryas–Preboreal abrupt warming event. Nature 548, 443–446 (2017)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e773">8</a></sup>, but that period is an imperfect analogue for present-day emissions owing to the large terrestrial ice sheet cover, lower sea level and extensive permafrost. Here we use preindustrial-era ice core <sup>14</sup>CH<sub>4</sub> measurements to show that natural geological CH<sub>4</sub> emissions to the atmosphere were about 1.6 teragrams CH<sub>4</sub> per year, with a maximum of 5.4 teragrams CH<sub>4</sub> per year (95 per cent confidence limit)—an order of magnitude lower than the currently used estimates. This result indicates that anthropogenic fossil CH<sub>4</sub> emissions are underestimated by about 38 to 58 teragrams CH<sub>4</sub> per year, or about 25 to 40 per cent of recent estimates. Our record highlights the human impact on the atmosphere and climate, provides a firm target for inventories of the global CH<sub>4</sub> budget, and will help to inform strategies for targeted emission reductions<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 9" title="Höglund-Isaksson, L. Global anthropogenic methane emissions 2005–2030: technical mitigation potentials and costs. Atmos. Chem. Phys. 12, 9079–9096 (2012)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR9" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e794">9</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 10" title="Howarth, R. W. Methane emissions and climatic warming risk from hydraulic fracturing and shale gas development: implications for policy. Eng. Emis. Con. Tech. 3, 45–54 (2015)." href="/articles/s41586-020-1991-8#ref-CR10" id="ref-link-section-d33610347e797">10</a></sup>.</p></div></div></section>

            
                
